      "As for when it is connected to the wāw of plurality, examples are: \"The sincere men are those who perform their duty\" and \"You—O people—perform your duty.\" The words \"they stand\" and \"you stand\" are mudāriʿ verbs in the state of rafʿ. The sign of them being marfūʿ is the presence of the letter nūn and the letter wāw as the plural subject, non-inflectable upon a sukūn in the marfūʿ state. From this it can be seen that the mudāriʿ verb connected to this letter wāw will sometimes commence with the letter yā to indicate the third person—as in the first example—and sometimes commence with the letter tā to indicate the second person—as in the second example.",
